Markdown# 📘 Git & GitHub – Complete Notes (Beginner to Advanced)## 📌 1. What is Git?**Git** is a **Distributed Version Control System (DVCS)** used to:- Track changes in source code- Collaborate with teams- Maintain history of a project- Revert to older versions if something breaks### ✅ Key Characteristics- Distributed (each developer has full copy)- Fast and reliable- Supports branching & merging- Open source---## 📌 2. What is GitHub?**GitHub** is a **cloud-based platform** that hosts Git repositories.> **Git = tool**  > **GitHub = service/platform**### ✅ GitHub provides:- Remote repositories- Collaboration (Pull Requests)- Issue tracking- CI/CD integrations- Code review tools---## 📌 3. Git Architecture (Very Important)Git works with **3 main areas**:Show more lines
Working Directory  →  Staging Area  →  Local Repository

### ✅ Explanation

| Area | Purpose |
|------|--------|
| Working Directory | Files you edit |
| Staging Area (Index) | Files prepared for commit |
| Local Repository | Committed files (history) |

---

## 📌 4. Installing & Configuring Git

### 🔹 Check installation
```bash
git --version

🔹 Global configuration
Shellgit config --global user.name "Your Name"git config --global user.email "your@email.com"Show more lines
🔹 View configuration
Shellgit config --listShow more lines

📌 5. Creating a Git Repository
🔹 Initialize a repo
Shellgit initShow more lines

This creates a hidden .git folder.


📌 6. Git Basic Commands (Core Commands)
✅ Check repository status
Shellgit statusShow more lines
✅ Track new files
Shellgit add file.txtgit add .Show more lines
✅ Commit changes
Shellgit commit -m "Initial commit"Show more lines
✅ View commit history
Shellgit loggit log --onelineShow more lines

📌 7. Git Lifecycle
Untracked → Staged → Committed

🔹 Example
Shellgit add app.jsgit commit -m "Add app logic"Show more lines

📌 8. Ignoring Files (.gitignore)
Used to ignore files like:

node_modules
Logs
Env files

🔹 Example .gitignore
.ignorenode_modules/.env*.logShow more lines

📌 9. Branching in Git
🔹 What is a branch?
A branch is an independent line of development.
Default branch: main or master
🔹 List branches
Shellgit branchShow more lines
🔹 Create branch
Shellgit branch feature-loginShow more lines
🔹 Switch branch
Shellgit checkout feature-loginShow more lines
🔹 Create + switch
Shellgit checkout -b feature-loginShow more lines

📌 10. Merging Branches
🔹 Fast-forward Merge
When no divergence exists.
Shellgit checkout maingit merge feature-loginShow more lines
🔹 Three-way Merge
Occurs when both branches changed.

📌 11. Merge Conflict (Very Important)
✅ What is a merge conflict?
Occurs when same lines in same file are modified in different branches.
🔹 Conflict example
Plain Text<<<<<<< HEADconsole.log("Main branch");=======console.log("Feature branch");>>>>>>> feature-loginShow more lines
✅ How to resolve

Open file
Decide correct code
Remove conflict markers
Save file
Run:

Shellgit add .git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict"Show more lines

📌 12. Git Diff
Shows differences between changes.
Shellgit diffgit diff --stagedgit diff commit1 commit2Show more lines

📌 13. Undoing Things in Git
🔹 Unstage a file
Shellgit reset file.txtShow more lines
🔹 Discard changes
Shellgit checkout -- file.txtShow more lines
🔹 Amend last commit
Shellgit commit --amendShow more lines

📌 14. Git Reset vs Revert
✅ git reset (Dangerous – history changes)
Shellgit reset --soft HEAD~1git reset --mixed HEAD~1git reset --hard HEAD~1Show more lines
✅ git revert (Safe – recommended)
Shellgit revert <commit_hash>Show more lines

📌 15. Stashing (Temporary Save)
🔹 Save changes
Shellgit stashShow more lines
🔹 List stash
Shellgit stash listShow more lines
🔹 Apply stash
Shellgit stash applyShow more lines

📌 16. Git Tags
Used for releases.
Shellgit tag v1.0git taggit push origin v1.0Show more lines

📌 17. Remote Repositories (GitHub)
🔹 Add remote
Shellgit remote add origin https://github.com/user/repo.gitShow more lines
🔹 View remote
Shellgit remote -vShow more lines
🔹 Push code
Shellgit push origin mainShow more lines
🔹 Pull changes
Shellgit pull origin mainShow more lines

📌 18. Git Clone
Clone a GitHub repo:
Shellgit clone https://github.com/user/repo.gitShow more lines

📌 19. Git Fetch vs Pull

















CommandWhat it doesgit fetchDownloads changes onlygit pullFetch + merge

📌 20. GitHub Workflow (Real Project Flow)
✅ Team Workflow

Clone repo
Create feature branch
Work on code
Commit changes
Push branch
Create Pull Request
Code review
Merge to main


📌 21. Pull Requests (PR)
Used to:

Review code
Discuss changes
Merge safely

PR Flow:
Feature branch → Pull Request → Review → Merge


📌 22. Forking Workflow
Used in open-source projects.

Fork repo
Clone fork
Create branch
Push changes
Open PR to original repo


📌 23. Git Rebase
Rebase rewrites history.
Shellgit rebase mainShow more lines
✅ Merge vs Rebase

















MergeRebasePreserves historyLinear historySaferCleaner

📌 24. Cherry-pick
Apply single commit from another branch.
Shellgit cherry-pick <commit_hash>Show more lines

📌 25. Git Aliases
Shellgit config --global alias.co checkoutgit config --global alias.br branchgit config --global alias.cm "commit -m"git config --global alias.st statusgit config --global alias.lg "log --oneline --graph --decorate --all"Show more lines

📌 26. Git Best Practices

✅ Commit small changes
✅ Write meaningful commit messages
✅ Use branches
✅ Pull before push
✅ Do not commit secrets


📌 27. Common Git Errors & Fixes
❌ Push rejected
Shellgit pull --rebasegit pushShow more lines
❌ Detached HEAD (accidentally checked out a commit)
Shellgit checkout mainShow more lines

📌 28. Git Interview Quick Notes

Git is decentralized
HEAD points to current branch/commit
Merge conflict occurs on the same line
git pull = fetch + merge
revert is safer than reset